e two ways of getting around  right there's the the um system for  controlling the legs the limbs  but also a parallel system for  controlling the wings  and flies don't walk and fly at the same  time  but it seems likely that some of  the apparatus for planning and executing  navigational strategies  might be common between those pathways  so  my collaborator michael dickinson in our  nih brain initiative funded consortium  has been really doing a lot of work to  identify  the components of the navigation  apparatus  that works during flight and also how  sort of  interesting and specific motor control  problems are solved  in flight and so kind of like the common  theme of our project is loops in the  nervous system and  it's interesting to think that the loops  that control navigation walking  are also working in parallel with work  loops that control navigation and flight  how these things  how conflicts between them are resolved  and how they're integrated is a really  fascinating question  excellent so more to come maybe when we  do know the whole full connectome of the  of the fly you can integrate those two  systems  um there were a number of questions  around plasticity  um and i'm going to try to merge them  into one  the plasticity experiment in particular  where you uh have cells that emerge with  two receptive fields seem really  fascinating to me  and to a few other of the questioners so  what does that remodeling of the  receptive field tell you about the  functional role of those  epg neurons during active navigation and  how is it compatible  with the potential of those cells to  develop through plasticity to receptive  fields  right so i think the question um that  you asked josh is a related to this  experiment where we created  a virtual reality environment with two  bright objects  um 180 degrees apart you can think of it  as like the tatooine experiment there  are two suns in the sky  they just are on opposite sides of the  sky so we designed t